===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɪt/|a=RP,GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-git.ogg|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-git.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪt|s=1}}
* {{hmp|en|ghit}} {{q|one pronunciation}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|get||[illegitimate] offspring}}. A southern variant of {{cog|sco|get||illegitimate child, brat}}, related to {{m|en|beget}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Britain|Ireland|slang|pejorative}} A [[silly]], [[incompetent]], [[stupid]]{{,}} or [[annoying]] person (usually a man).
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1968|lyricist=w:John Lennon|composer=|title=w:I'm So Tired|album={{w|The Beatles (album)|The Beatles}}|artist=w:the Beatles|passage=Although I'm so tired, I'll have another cigarette / And curse Sir Walter Raleigh, he was such a stupid '''git'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=December 18 2000|title={{w|BBC}} and {{w|Bafta}} Tribute to {{w|Michael Caine}}|section=16:43-17:05
|passage=[[:w:Michael Parkinson|Parkinson]]: You made films before, but the part that really made your name was [[:w:Zulu (1964 film)|''Zulu'']], wasn't it {{...}} and there of course{{mdash}}against [[type]]{{mdash}}you played the [[toff]], you played the officer.<br>[[:w:Michael Caine|Caine]]: I played the officer, yeah, and everybody thought I was like that. Everyone was so shocked when they met me, this like [[Cockney]] guy had played this [[toffee-nosed]] '''git'''.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''Git'' is usually used as an insult, more severe than {{m|en|twit}} but less severe than a true profanity like {{m|en|wanker}} or {{m|en|arsehole}}, and may often be used affectionately between friends. ''Get'' can also be used, with a subtle change of meaning. "You cheeky get!" is slightly less harsh than "You cheeky git!".
* ''Git'' is frequently used in conjunction with another word to achieve a more specific meaning. For instance a "smarmy git" refers to a person of a slimy, ingratiating disposition; a "jammy git" would be a person with undeserved luck. The phrase "grumpy old git", denoting a cantankerous old man, is used with particular frequency.
* In parts of northern England, Ireland and Scotland, {{m|en|get}} is still used in preference to ''git''.
* The word has been ruled by the {{w|Speaker of the House of Commons (United Kingdom)|Speaker of the House of Commons}} to be {{w|unparliamentary language}}.<ref>{{cite-book|url=http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=tN7WAAAAMAAJ|title=An encyclopedia of swearing|isbn=9780765612311|year=2006|author=Geoffrey Hughes|page=477}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|url= http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=awERFb65TuwC|year=2006|isbn=9781843171621|title=Joy of Swearing|author=M. Hunt, Alison Maloney}}</ref>

